4725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74aa14ec-4817-573b-816c-aa9e7cef4458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1061 36491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b782d57f-6faa-597a-9408-0b6fc960adfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1061 36619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d050b8f3-0cc1-593c-8200-076f5b4f824f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1061 36620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fded71-c2d5-5d66-9f13-5b0253efd6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1061 4327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38f3ace0-f394-598e-8dad-37fefda9d86e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1061 4328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "459089fc-d278-5487-bc0b-e4cd6237c142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}$.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5$, respectively.\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{5} + 4\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(2 + 4)\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n2\\sqrt{7} - 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n(2 - 1)\\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n1 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1065 1609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aa49408-30d5-57b5-8dff-ec1744f9fc6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1065 5995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4af2af2e-0f81-5094-adf6-2ede72a493d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt[3]{2} - 6\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n(4 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n&= - 2\\sqrt[3]{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5$.\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1065 5996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32078f96-6eaf-57d2-bc71-8af8495d5d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
